  no module system, no router. So "other forms later" most likely means [3mother pages[0m, and that rules some options in and
  out.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how long the stored [38;5;153muserId[39m survives, which is a straight trade between reach and residue.

  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives page navigation, tab close, and browser restart. Any other page on the origin can read it.
    This is the only option that still works if someone closes the tab and comes back. The cost is residue: on a shared
    or kiosk machine the last user's ID sits on disk until something explicitly clears it, so a "clear on logout" path
    becomes part of the design rather than an afterthought.
  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — survives navigation between your pages within a tab, dies when the tab closes. Covers "other forms
    later" for a normal multi-page flow, and the residue problem largely disappears on its own. Doesn't survive a
    restart.
  - [1mIn-memory module variable[0m — dies on every page load. Given there's no router here, this fails your "other forms will
    need it" requirement outright. I'm listing it so you can see why I'm not recommending it.

  [1mMy recommendation: [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m.[0m It reaches every other page in the flow, which is the requirement you actually
  stated, and it expires by itself, which means the correlation ID can't outlive the visit on a shared machine.
  [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m buys you persistence across restarts, and for analytics correlation I don't think you need that — but if
  you're trying to stitch together [3mreturn visits[0m, that's a real reason to pick it, and you should say so.

  Both are a one-line swap behind the small accessor module I'm going to propose, so this is cheap to change later — the
  part that isn't cheap is the module boundary, which is the next thing I'll show you.
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[38;5;16m[48;5;153m ☐ Persistence [39m[49m

[1m[38;5;231mHow long should the stored userId survive?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153msessionStorage (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mReaches other pages in the same tab, dies on tab close. No residue on shared machines. Doesn't survive a browser [39m
     [38;5;246mrestart.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m localStorage
     [38;5;246mSurvives restarts, so return visits correlate. Needs an explicit clear-on-logout path so one user's ID doesn't leak
[39m     [38;5;246minto the next user's session.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m In-memory only
     [38;5;246mSimplest, but resets on every page load, so other pages won't see it. Only viable if this stays a single page.[39m
